  "notes": "The neotraditional radio producer. Keys are documented as a decision with a documented counter-example: \"guitar keys with G major dominating the catalogue he is best known for; keys are set high enough to keep the vocal bright — documented by contrast, since a different producer's 2006 record on the same artist deliberately lowered the keys below his usual range to change the delivery.\" Harmony \"stays inside I–IV–V with V7 and blues turns,\" so the seventh share carries the dominant colour and the numerals stay degree-only. Drums are \"standard Nashville live tracking with a dry, un-gated 2&4; two-beat on uptempo cuts, cross-stick on ballads,\" and the entry is candid that his signature is casting and arrangement rather than mic technique — which is why the identifiable parameter here is structural: \"an unusually long instrumental section — his flagship artist wanted extended breaks suitable for dancing, so build 8- or 16-bar instrumental passages with the Tele and steel trading, rather than a single 4-bar solo.\" That is the long bridge slot and the high counter density. The bass is \"staccato two-beat root-fifth on uptempo cuts, tied notes on ballads; no programmed low end,\" hence no `bass808` block and near-zero anticipation. Form has two stated negatives: \"no key changes, no ritards.\" ⚠ Confidence is medium and the research says why: \"the long-instrumental habit and the vocal-mic constancy are documented; the rest is read off the catalogue and the Nashville board [LOW-CONF].\" ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, the perc density and the counter ratio.",
